WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.229 --> 00:00:06.589
So the Blackboard component is our equivalent of the behavior tree component for the behavior tree,

2
00:00:06.589 --> 00:00:08.589
the Blackboard component is to the Blackboard.

3
00:00:08.589 --> 00:00:11.470
It's a wrapper around the kind of core object,

4
00:00:11.470 --> 00:00:15.630
so in this case the Blackboard is the thing that the Blackboard component holds inside it,

5
00:00:15.630 --> 00:00:18.670
and it gives us a way to interact with that Blackboard from outside it,

6
00:00:18.670 --> 00:00:21.870
so we can pass information in, we can query information out of it.

7
00:00:21.870 --> 00:00:25.390
Just as an example if we were to jump to our project,

8
00:00:25.390 --> 00:00:29.550
if we go into the AI controller, again this is the project you can download with this tutorial,

9
00:00:30.670 --> 00:00:33.469
we don't need to go into this in detail at the moment,

10
00:00:33.469 --> 00:00:36.509
I'll demonstrate this a little bit later on what this is all actually doing,

11
00:00:36.509 --> 00:00:41.310
but if we look inside our AI controller when we perceive some actors,

12
00:00:41.310 --> 00:00:43.630
we do a little bit of querying, we check if it's a pawn,

13
00:00:43.630 --> 00:00:47.390
and then this point where we do Blackboard set value as object,

14
00:00:47.390 --> 00:00:52.509
this is reaching into the Blackboard component on the AI controller and setting a value.

15
00:00:52.509 --> 00:00:54.750
So this is reaching into our Blackboard,

16
00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:58.350
assigning something based on world logic from world data,

17
00:00:58.909 --> 00:01:01.229
and then it is assigned to the Blackboard,

18
00:01:01.229 --> 00:01:05.310
and then the behavior tree which is linked with this Blackboard

19
00:01:05.310 --> 00:01:06.989
can operate with that information,

20
00:01:06.989 --> 00:01:12.590
can behave based on that gameplay change, based on that world data.

21
00:01:13.390 --> 00:01:17.310
So as I mentioned it's strongly paired with the behavior tree component,

22
00:01:18.269 --> 00:01:22.109
this is really obvious from even just when you go into editing a Blackboard,

23
00:01:22.109 --> 00:01:25.069
if I go to find our behavior tree here,

24
00:01:26.239 --> 00:01:27.519
and I go to our Blackboard,

25
00:01:28.079 --> 00:01:30.799
well okay from the Blackboard maybe it's,

26
00:01:30.799 --> 00:01:34.400
but if you go to your behavior tree this is linked with a Blackboard asset,

27
00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:36.319
and you can jump back and forth between the two,

28
00:01:36.319 --> 00:01:39.040
we've got the editor kind of like, you know,

29
00:01:40.019 --> 00:01:42.819
really indicates how strongly these two things are paired together,

30
00:01:42.819 --> 00:01:45.140
but even inside the actual code itself,

31
00:01:45.140 --> 00:01:51.379
if we were to jump to our Blackboard component,

32
00:01:52.099 --> 00:02:01.569
there we see we've got functions for getting the brain component,

33
00:02:01.569 --> 00:02:03.730
we've got a function for caching the brain component,

34
00:02:03.730 --> 00:02:06.129
so it very much expects the thing to exist,

35
00:02:06.129 --> 00:02:10.050
and even inside our brain component,

36
00:02:10.050 --> 00:02:12.289
I think it is, I don't think it's the behavior tree,

37
00:02:12.289 --> 00:02:13.650
it might be the behavior tree, let's see,

38
00:02:15.409 --> 00:02:23.620
behavior tree component.cpp, sorry,

39
00:02:26.500 --> 00:02:38.740
and I think it's in the initialize component cache behavior,

40
00:02:40.419 --> 00:02:43.460
or maybe it's inside the brain component,

41
00:02:43.460 --> 00:02:44.020
one second,

42
00:02:44.979 --> 00:02:52.000
braincomponent.cpp, yeah right there,

43
00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:55.840
so inside our brain component on the initialize component,

44
00:02:55.840 --> 00:02:59.759
it expects that, you know, whatever owns this component

45
00:02:59.759 --> 00:03:01.840
also has a Blackboard component class,

46
00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:05.919
and then it kind of assigns itself as the brain for that Blackboard,

47
00:03:05.919 --> 00:03:09.199
so you can see how much these two things expect

48
00:03:09.199 --> 00:03:11.199
to kind of work in conjunction with each other.

49
00:03:13.819 --> 00:03:16.219
So yeah, in terms of the actual creation,

50
00:03:16.219 --> 00:03:18.860
it follows a similar pattern to the behavior tree,

51
00:03:18.860 --> 00:03:22.460
so you can already create one on your AI controller,

52
00:03:23.340 --> 00:03:25.180
and then it's kind of already available

53
00:03:25.180 --> 00:03:28.300
if you want the AI to work as soon as it's possessing an object,

54
00:03:29.419 --> 00:03:31.580
and similarly, if you go to run a behavior tree,

55
00:03:31.580 --> 00:03:34.139
what that will do is, as you've seen,

56
00:03:34.139 --> 00:03:39.099
you can choose a default Blackboard to use for your behavior tree,

57
00:03:40.699 --> 00:03:43.500
you can just assign the Blackboard this should use,

58
00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:45.900
so if you were to run that behavior tree,

59
00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:49.340
and the AI controller didn't have a behavior tree component already,

60
00:03:49.340 --> 00:03:51.419
it would create the appropriate behavior tree component,

61
00:03:51.419 --> 00:03:52.699
plug in this behavior tree,

62
00:03:52.699 --> 00:03:54.300
and then create a Blackboard component

63
00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:56.860
using the default Blackboard of that behavior tree.

64
00:03:58.539 --> 00:04:01.340
So essentially you can create the Blackboard in two ways,

65
00:04:01.340 --> 00:04:03.020
you can make it ahead of time in editor,

66
00:04:03.020 --> 00:04:05.659
or you can rely on that kind of again created at runtime

67
00:04:05.659 --> 00:04:08.060
by that run behavior tree functionality.

68
00:04:11.300 --> 00:04:14.099
And then, oh yeah, the last thing to mention is just that

69
00:04:14.819 --> 00:04:16.980
one of the kind of powerful things about Blackboard

70
00:04:16.980 --> 00:04:21.459
is the ability for objects to kind of register themselves as observers,

71
00:04:21.459 --> 00:04:23.459
and what you would essentially do is you,

72
00:04:24.259 --> 00:04:27.379
Blackboard keys are again this kind of variable type,

73
00:04:27.379 --> 00:04:29.459
so it represents particular data,

74
00:04:29.459 --> 00:04:32.180
but it's kind of a struct around that data,

75
00:04:32.180 --> 00:04:35.379
and you register yourself as a listener for a particular Blackboard key

76
00:04:35.379 --> 00:04:37.939
to be notified when that Blackboard key has changed.

77
00:04:37.939 --> 00:04:41.459
So an example of that is the move to execute task,

78
00:04:42.899 --> 00:04:45.379
I think I've got that open still,

79
00:04:46.579 --> 00:04:47.779
bt task move to,

80
00:04:49.139 --> 00:04:54.449
and if we go to execute task,

81
00:04:55.009 --> 00:04:56.769
you'll see here,

82
00:04:57.519 --> 00:04:59.519
it reaches into the Blackboard component,

83
00:05:01.040 --> 00:05:01.759
gets the memory,

84
00:05:01.759 --> 00:05:03.439
gets the delegate handle,

85
00:05:03.439 --> 00:05:07.220
and basically registers an observer like this.

86
00:05:09.459 --> 00:05:12.019
So essentially, in this case,

87
00:05:12.019 --> 00:05:14.899
it's the move to task might need to be notified

88
00:05:14.899 --> 00:05:16.500
if the Blackboard value has changed,

89
00:05:17.779 --> 00:05:20.180
either because the thing it's following has changed,

90
00:05:20.180 --> 00:05:23.779
or if the actor has moved,

91
00:05:23.779 --> 00:05:25.459
we need to be kind of aware of that.

92
00:05:27.139 --> 00:05:29.860
And other examples of that would be,

93
00:05:29.860 --> 00:05:33.379
as we've kind of already mentioned in our behavior tree,

94
00:05:34.100 --> 00:05:36.339
we can have a decorator

95
00:05:36.339 --> 00:05:38.500
that's kind of based on a Blackboard condition,

96
00:05:38.500 --> 00:05:41.699
and so therefore this will need to register itself

97
00:05:41.699 --> 00:05:44.500
as an observer of whatever Blackboard key we choose,

98
00:05:44.500 --> 00:05:46.500
in this case it's target actor,

99
00:05:46.500 --> 00:05:48.180
so that this Blackboard,

100
00:05:48.180 --> 00:05:52.259
so that this decorator can essentially be like rerun,

101
00:05:52.259 --> 00:05:53.620
can abort,

102
00:05:53.620 --> 00:05:55.939
can do whatever abort behavior it needs to do,

103
00:05:55.939 --> 00:05:58.740
can rerun to check for whether or not the result has changed.

104
00:05:59.699 --> 00:06:02.980
So yeah, that's another place where this logic

105
00:06:03.459 --> 00:06:06.019
registering as an observer will already be getting utilized.

106
00:06:07.470 --> 00:06:10.110
So we've talked about the kind of behavior logic,

107
00:06:10.829 --> 00:06:13.069
we've talked about the brain, the memory,

108
00:06:13.069 --> 00:06:17.709
but one of the really key components to implementing AI

109
00:06:17.709 --> 00:06:19.790
is getting the AI to move around.

110
00:06:19.790 --> 00:06:26.430
Unreal has its own native implementation of 3D navigation,

111
00:06:27.629 --> 00:06:29.389
which is the navigation system.

112
00:06:29.949 --> 00:06:33.790
So the navigation system is comprised of a mesh,

113
00:06:33.790 --> 00:06:35.069
which is generated in editor,

114
00:06:36.430 --> 00:06:41.230
and essentially this mesh will query things about the environment

115
00:06:41.230 --> 00:06:46.110
and generate a mesh either at runtime or editor time,

116
00:06:46.110 --> 00:06:47.870
typically editor time first,

117
00:06:48.990 --> 00:06:55.550
to basically do a lot of tracing and querying in a kind of geometric sense

118
00:06:56.350 --> 00:06:59.790
to establish what areas are navigable and non-navigable.

119
00:07:00.350 --> 00:07:01.870
It's important to mention that this is

120
00:07:03.230 --> 00:07:06.269
particularly for entities that run along the ground.

121
00:07:06.990 --> 00:07:10.189
So if we wanted something like 3D,

122
00:07:11.149 --> 00:07:15.149
so either for flying around or swimming based navigation,

123
00:07:15.709 --> 00:07:18.910
it's likely that you'll need to roll your own implementation for that.

124
00:07:18.910 --> 00:07:20.589
It's always worth checking Unreal plugins,

125
00:07:20.589 --> 00:07:22.029
it's always worth looking at FAB

126
00:07:22.029 --> 00:07:25.230
because it's quite likely that something for this already exists,

127
00:07:26.110 --> 00:07:28.910
but certainly the out-of-the-box navigation system,

128
00:07:28.910 --> 00:07:29.949
the nav mesh,

129
00:07:29.949 --> 00:07:33.149
is designed for along-the-floor land-based AI movement.

130
00:07:34.100 --> 00:07:37.060
As I said, it already supports static and dynamic,

131
00:07:37.060 --> 00:07:42.980
so we can generate this nav data at editor time,

132
00:07:42.980 --> 00:07:45.139
or if we need it to be responsive to things

133
00:07:45.139 --> 00:07:46.420
that are changing in the environment,

134
00:07:46.420 --> 00:07:47.939
we can mark it up as dynamic,

135
00:07:47.939 --> 00:07:49.379
obviously with a slight performance cost.

136
00:07:50.899 --> 00:07:53.379
And then we can also use nav modifier volumes,

137
00:07:53.379 --> 00:07:55.540
which can be used as part of this,

138
00:07:56.180 --> 00:07:58.339
either statically or dynamically,

139
00:07:58.339 --> 00:08:01.699
and these can incur navigation costs

140
00:08:01.699 --> 00:08:03.860
if we want areas of high cost,

141
00:08:03.860 --> 00:08:06.100
and they can also act as kind of blockers

142
00:08:06.100 --> 00:08:09.779
to prevent navigation being generated around particular areas.

143
00:08:10.899 --> 00:08:13.220
Physical objects can also affect this in a similar way.

144
00:08:14.100 --> 00:08:16.259
Anytime you're creating a primitive component,

145
00:08:16.259 --> 00:08:20.420
so typically a kind of collision or a visual mesh,

146
00:08:21.139 --> 00:08:22.500
you can mark that object

147
00:08:22.500 --> 00:08:24.660
as whether or not it should affect navigation,

148
00:08:24.660 --> 00:08:27.060
and then that would be whether or not essentially

149
00:08:27.060 --> 00:08:29.459
the navigation system should pay attention to this mesh,

150
00:08:29.459 --> 00:08:31.860
to its bounding volume,

151
00:08:32.899 --> 00:08:34.019
whether it should pay attention to it

152
00:08:34.019 --> 00:08:36.179
when it's generating navigable space.

153
00:08:38.240 --> 00:08:39.919
Navigation can be generated,

154
00:08:39.919 --> 00:08:42.559
different navigation can be generated for different agents,

155
00:08:42.559 --> 00:08:46.080
so we can have the idea of large objects and small objects,

156
00:08:46.080 --> 00:08:47.840
and then navigating in different ways,

157
00:08:47.840 --> 00:08:49.840
being able to negotiate different spaces,

158
00:08:50.240 --> 00:08:53.600
a motorbike can get through a gap that a tank can't,

159
00:08:53.600 --> 00:08:56.240
and so we can generate different nav meshes

160
00:08:56.879 --> 00:08:58.480
to kind of like account for that.

161
00:08:59.440 --> 00:09:02.399
We can also, just whenever we're talking about nav mesh stuff,

162
00:09:02.399 --> 00:09:04.159
it's worth mentioning that we can hit P

163
00:09:04.159 --> 00:09:06.080
whenever we're in editor to visualize this.

164
00:09:07.440 --> 00:09:10.879
This is not when you're in player and editor mode,

165
00:09:12.480 --> 00:09:14.159
or it can be when you're in player and editor mode,

166
00:09:14.159 --> 00:09:15.519
but only when you're ejected,

167
00:09:15.519 --> 00:09:16.799
so not when you're playing as a,

168
00:09:17.360 --> 00:09:18.879
possessing a pawn moving around,

169
00:09:19.679 --> 00:09:22.240
but you can just press P to kind of see the nav mesh

170
00:09:22.240 --> 00:09:23.200
and how it's being generated.

171
00:09:23.200 --> 00:09:25.279
That's either at editor time or at runtime

172
00:09:25.279 --> 00:09:26.320
if you're in eject mode.

173
00:09:27.549 --> 00:09:29.950
And also, yeah, we can form links

174
00:09:29.950 --> 00:09:32.669
between different kind of islands of nav mesh

175
00:09:32.669 --> 00:09:34.990
to allow AI to kind of traverse spaces.

176
00:09:35.629 --> 00:09:37.950
This can either be from heights,

177
00:09:37.950 --> 00:09:38.909
so we can, you know,

178
00:09:38.909 --> 00:09:42.590
we can handle the idea of like jumping from a ledge to the ground,

179
00:09:42.590 --> 00:09:44.110
or it can be, as I said,

180
00:09:44.110 --> 00:09:48.509
through kind of areas of distinct space

181
00:09:48.909 --> 00:09:50.830
that have been generated to kind of,

182
00:09:50.830 --> 00:09:52.429
we can link them together

183
00:09:52.429 --> 00:09:54.429
so that AI can traverse between them.

184
00:09:56.289 --> 00:09:57.730
So as we've already mentioned,

185
00:09:57.730 --> 00:09:59.570
we've kind of covered the kind of bare bones,

186
00:09:59.570 --> 00:10:01.570
but just to kind of go into like actually

187
00:10:01.570 --> 00:10:02.850
how we would do this

188
00:10:02.850 --> 00:10:05.330
to actually make a nav mesh in the first place,

189
00:10:05.330 --> 00:10:07.090
if we were just to start a new map.

190
00:10:09.940 --> 00:10:11.860
By default, when you make an unreal level,

191
00:10:12.500 --> 00:10:13.620
it won't have nav,

192
00:10:14.500 --> 00:10:16.659
but all you have to do to create it

193
00:10:16.659 --> 00:10:22.580
is if you go to volumes and go to nav mesh bounds volume.

194
00:10:23.620 --> 00:10:25.700
And as soon as you create that,

195
00:10:25.700 --> 00:10:27.940
you'll see you get this recast nav mesh.

196
00:10:28.580 --> 00:10:31.139
This essentially is the runtime data

197
00:10:31.139 --> 00:10:33.860
that represents your navigable space,

198
00:10:34.659 --> 00:10:37.299
whereas it's the volumes that mark out areas

199
00:10:37.299 --> 00:10:38.740
that should be navigable.

200
00:10:38.740 --> 00:10:40.820
So if I drop that here,

201
00:10:40.820 --> 00:10:42.899
you can see the nav mesh is being generated.

202
00:10:42.899 --> 00:10:44.899
You can see the effect of hitting P here.

203
00:10:44.899 --> 00:10:47.299
That's because I was already in show navigation mode.

204
00:10:47.299 --> 00:10:48.419
So if you press P,

205
00:10:48.419 --> 00:10:49.940
this is what it would be toggling on and off.

206
00:10:49.940 --> 00:10:51.220
We get this kind of like data

207
00:10:51.220 --> 00:10:52.980
about the nav mesh that have been generated.

208
00:10:52.980 --> 00:10:55.059
We can see that mesh being generated.

209
00:10:55.059 --> 00:10:55.860
And you can see,

210
00:10:57.379 --> 00:11:00.899
if I were to just like expand this a little bit

211
00:11:00.899 --> 00:11:01.940
or something like that,

212
00:11:01.940 --> 00:11:03.620
that's going to marry up there.

213
00:11:03.620 --> 00:11:06.419
And I can also just duplicate this bounds volume

214
00:11:06.419 --> 00:11:07.700
if I want to say like,

215
00:11:07.700 --> 00:11:09.539
this is navigable space and that.

216
00:11:09.539 --> 00:11:11.139
And you'll notice that even though

217
00:11:11.139 --> 00:11:14.019
I'm making multiple nav mesh bounds volumes,

218
00:11:14.019 --> 00:11:17.220
I'm not getting more recast nav mesh data.

219
00:11:17.860 --> 00:11:22.779
That's because this is our kind of like global idea

220
00:11:22.779 --> 00:11:24.779
of our navigable space.

221
00:11:24.779 --> 00:11:28.700
And the volumes represent each of the kind of spaces

222
00:11:28.700 --> 00:11:30.620
that we want to demark as navigable space.

223
00:11:30.620 --> 00:11:32.460
This is basically our paintbrush.

224
00:11:32.460 --> 00:11:34.700
And then this is actually really the thing

225
00:11:34.700 --> 00:11:36.220
that represents our runtime data

226
00:11:36.220 --> 00:11:40.059
of the fully built up picture of all our navigable space.

227
00:11:43.580 --> 00:11:44.620
And then there's, yeah,

228
00:11:44.620 --> 00:11:47.419
once we've got this nav mesh,

229
00:11:47.419 --> 00:11:49.419
there's a couple of different settings we can tweak on it.

230
00:11:49.419 --> 00:11:53.019
So it's in the volumes again,

231
00:11:53.019 --> 00:11:54.860
are just there to define areas

232
00:11:54.860 --> 00:11:57.500
that should be considered for navigation.

233
00:11:57.500 --> 00:12:00.460
And then it's up to the settings on our nav mesh,

234
00:12:00.460 --> 00:12:01.899
on our recast nav mesh

235
00:12:03.340 --> 00:12:05.100
to kind of define various things

236
00:12:05.100 --> 00:12:06.860
about how that nav mesh is being generated.

237
00:12:06.860 --> 00:12:08.539
So there's settings in here

238
00:12:08.539 --> 00:12:12.460
such as being able to tweak our resolutions,

239
00:12:12.460 --> 00:12:14.379
being able to tweak our agent radius.

240
00:12:14.379 --> 00:12:17.580
We can tweak our cell sizes.

241
00:12:17.580 --> 00:12:21.100
So if I were to put a block,

242
00:12:22.860 --> 00:12:26.220
if I would just create, I don't know, a cone or something.

243
00:12:28.740 --> 00:12:30.019
No idea where that cone just went.

244
00:12:30.019 --> 00:12:31.220
Just going to try making that again.

245
00:12:36.179 --> 00:12:40.259
Okay, I don't know why it's suddenly having an issue creating.

246
00:12:40.259 --> 00:12:41.460
I'm going to try one more.

247
00:12:41.539 --> 00:12:42.899
I'm just going to try a cube.

248
00:12:42.899 --> 00:12:44.179
Okay, for some reason it likes cubes

249
00:12:44.179 --> 00:12:45.139
and doesn't like cylinders.

250
00:12:46.340 --> 00:12:49.539
So if I were to go to this nav mesh

251
00:12:49.539 --> 00:12:54.580
and then I can go to my tile size.

252
00:12:55.379 --> 00:12:57.220
Ah, I always forget exactly.

253
00:13:02.620 --> 00:13:04.379
Tile size on real units.

254
00:13:06.210 --> 00:13:07.490
Is that going to be right?

255
00:13:08.129 --> 00:13:10.429
If I change that to like 50.

256
00:13:10.429 --> 00:13:19.519
It's not in our bounds volume.

