Yeah, it is in the recast nav mesh.

258
00:13:25.200 --> 00:13:26.320
Oh, it's our cell sizes.

259
00:13:26.320 --> 00:13:26.799
There we go.

260
00:13:26.799 --> 00:13:28.080
Okay, so if I were to,

261
00:13:32.059 --> 00:13:39.259
we could change our cell size from like 38 down to something like one.

262
00:13:40.059 --> 00:13:42.779
And if I change that on all our settings,

263
00:13:42.779 --> 00:13:44.779
you can see it regenerates the nav mesh

264
00:13:44.779 --> 00:13:51.039
and we end up with a kind of more a kind of a nav mesh

265
00:13:51.039 --> 00:13:55.679
that more closely mimics or matches our geometry.

266
00:13:55.679 --> 00:13:58.159
Now obviously this is down to the fact

267
00:13:58.159 --> 00:14:00.159
that there's a kind of gap between our cube here

268
00:14:00.159 --> 00:14:01.679
is down to our agent radius.

269
00:14:01.679 --> 00:14:04.879
The whole point is this recast nav mesh

270
00:14:04.879 --> 00:14:06.960
is bound to a particular kind of agent

271
00:14:06.960 --> 00:14:09.200
and therefore it's generating the space

272
00:14:09.200 --> 00:14:11.759
that that kind of agent can navigate.

273
00:14:11.840 --> 00:14:18.100
And therefore, because our agent radius is whatever it is,

274
00:14:18.100 --> 00:14:19.620
35 by default,

275
00:14:20.659 --> 00:14:24.019
this is going to be like a 35 centimeter gap between the,

276
00:14:24.019 --> 00:14:25.539
or 35 unreal units,

277
00:14:25.539 --> 00:14:28.580
which is essentially centimeters gap between the geometry.

278
00:14:28.580 --> 00:14:29.860
So if I were to shrink that,

279
00:14:32.100 --> 00:14:34.740
you'd see kind of more closely hugs the geometry.

280
00:14:34.740 --> 00:14:35.940
And similarly again,

281
00:14:35.940 --> 00:14:39.539
because this is all these nav mesh bounds volumes

282
00:14:39.539 --> 00:14:41.379
are to say look everywhere.

283
00:14:41.460 --> 00:14:44.179
You could possibly here to generate nav.

284
00:14:44.179 --> 00:14:46.500
It's also generating it on top of spaces.

285
00:14:46.500 --> 00:14:48.980
It doesn't necessarily mean they'll be able to get here.

286
00:14:48.980 --> 00:14:51.700
There's no connection between these two islands,

287
00:14:51.700 --> 00:14:55.620
but these are both treated as independently possible navigable space.

288
00:14:56.419 --> 00:14:57.460
If I did want to connect this,

289
00:14:58.019 --> 00:14:59.379
we can talk about nav proxy links,

290
00:14:59.379 --> 00:15:00.419
which we can use in a little bit.

291
00:15:00.980 --> 00:15:06.450
Or the other thing we could do is introduce a slope

292
00:15:06.450 --> 00:15:07.970
to kind of be able to navigate up there,

293
00:15:07.970 --> 00:15:11.330
because one of the things we can also define in this nav mesh

294
00:15:11.330 --> 00:15:14.529
is our agent max slope.

295
00:15:15.090 --> 00:15:17.730
And we can see if I were to,

296
00:15:17.730 --> 00:15:18.690
for example,

297
00:15:19.490 --> 00:15:22.049
duplicate that cube.

298
00:15:22.049 --> 00:15:25.250
And you can see it's actually going to try

299
00:15:25.250 --> 00:15:27.809
and I'm going to just scale this up a little bit.

300
00:15:27.809 --> 00:15:30.289
You can see it starts generating mesh.

301
00:15:30.289 --> 00:15:32.289
And now that I've kind of got an acceptable slope,

302
00:15:32.289 --> 00:15:33.250
linking those two things,

303
00:15:33.250 --> 00:15:36.769
it's made one coherent navigable space.

304
00:15:37.870 --> 00:15:40.190
So that's how we create nav volumes.

305
00:15:40.190 --> 00:15:42.429
It's worth looking through everything available

306
00:15:42.429 --> 00:15:44.830
in your recast nav mesh

307
00:15:44.830 --> 00:15:46.509
to see the different settings available.

308
00:15:46.509 --> 00:15:49.309
If you're wondering why there are two recast nav meshes,

309
00:15:49.309 --> 00:15:51.230
that's because I have two profiles set up

310
00:15:51.230 --> 00:15:52.830
for different kind of agents.

311
00:15:52.830 --> 00:15:54.990
We'll get to those profiles in a little bit.

312
00:15:54.990 --> 00:15:57.789
But typically, if you just make a new project out of the box,

313
00:15:57.789 --> 00:15:58.909
you would only see one

314
00:15:58.909 --> 00:16:01.470
and it would probably be called recast nav mesh default.

315
00:16:01.470 --> 00:16:03.309
I think that's the default agent profile name.

316
00:16:04.669 --> 00:16:08.750
But yeah, it's worth looking at those recast nav mesh details

317
00:16:08.750 --> 00:16:10.669
to kind of see what's adjustable there.

318
00:16:13.299 --> 00:16:14.980
Oh, actually, just whilst we're on that,

319
00:16:14.980 --> 00:16:16.340
one last thing that I should mention

320
00:16:16.340 --> 00:16:19.620
is it's also in this nav mesh data

321
00:16:19.620 --> 00:16:20.580
that you would be choosing

322
00:16:20.580 --> 00:16:24.659
whether or not this data is static or dynamic.

323
00:16:24.659 --> 00:16:27.620
So I think it's one of the settings right at the bottom.

324
00:16:29.460 --> 00:16:33.379
You have a setting here, runtime generation.

325
00:16:33.379 --> 00:16:36.740
So by default, this nav mesh is going to be static,

326
00:16:36.820 --> 00:16:39.940
meaning it's not going to respond to runtime changes.

327
00:16:41.700 --> 00:16:45.299
Whereas if you were to say dynamic on dynamic modifiers only,

328
00:16:45.299 --> 00:16:48.820
we'll talk about nav modifiers in a little bit.

329
00:16:48.820 --> 00:16:50.740
But dynamic is going to be,

330
00:16:50.740 --> 00:16:52.820
this can respond to runtime changes

331
00:16:52.820 --> 00:16:54.419
and dynamic modifiers only is going to be,

332
00:16:54.419 --> 00:16:55.700
it's mostly static,

333
00:16:55.700 --> 00:16:59.940
but specifically nav modifiers can have a dynamic impact on this nav mesh.

334
00:17:00.740 --> 00:17:04.579
So that's a particularly important setting to be aware of

335
00:17:04.579 --> 00:17:08.900
if you need dynamic navigable spaces,

336
00:17:08.900 --> 00:17:10.339
you just know that that setting is there

337
00:17:10.339 --> 00:17:11.299
and that's how you would change that.

338
00:17:11.299 --> 00:17:13.779
And that is in the recast nav mesh data,

339
00:17:13.779 --> 00:17:15.460
not the nav volumes.

340
00:17:19.410 --> 00:17:20.930
Yep, so that's just kind of a little preview

341
00:17:20.930 --> 00:17:22.210
of what a nav mesh might look like.

342
00:17:22.210 --> 00:17:24.130
And again, similarly, you can see here,

343
00:17:24.130 --> 00:17:26.210
because this has geometry

344
00:17:26.210 --> 00:17:29.170
and because this geometry is marked up to affect navigation,

345
00:17:30.529 --> 00:17:32.049
that's why it's carving out a volume,

346
00:17:32.049 --> 00:17:35.009
this cube is either too far above the nav mesh

347
00:17:35.009 --> 00:17:38.769
or it's not marked to affect navigation.

348
00:17:38.769 --> 00:17:40.769
We can actually see that effect very quickly here.

349
00:17:40.769 --> 00:17:43.329
If I were to do can affect,

350
00:17:44.049 --> 00:17:45.410
you can see there's this tick box here.

351
00:17:45.410 --> 00:17:49.250
This is going to be true of pretty much any primitive components.

352
00:17:49.250 --> 00:17:54.049
So that's going to be box volumes, static meshes, anything like that.

353
00:17:54.049 --> 00:17:55.009
It's going to have that setting.

354
00:17:55.009 --> 00:17:56.769
And you can see as soon as I tick that off,

355
00:17:56.769 --> 00:17:58.849
it's no longer considering this mesh.

356
00:17:58.849 --> 00:18:01.410
It's going to generate navigable space underneath it.

357
00:18:01.410 --> 00:18:04.130
It doesn't mean the actors won't collide with this geometry,

358
00:18:04.130 --> 00:18:05.890
but certainly the navigation system

359
00:18:05.890 --> 00:18:08.369
isn't going to consider it for ignoring,

360
00:18:09.170 --> 00:18:11.569
take that into account when generating the navigable space.

361
00:18:13.140 --> 00:18:15.140
So agent profiles, I kind of already mentioned,

362
00:18:15.140 --> 00:18:17.059
but I'll just quickly show those off.

363
00:18:18.180 --> 00:18:20.180
As we said, where by default,

364
00:18:20.180 --> 00:18:21.940
there'll just be one profile set up for you.

365
00:18:21.940 --> 00:18:25.220
But I believe if you go into your edit project settings

366
00:18:26.420 --> 00:18:29.380
and into engine navigation system,

367
00:18:30.740 --> 00:18:32.420
we have supported agents.

368
00:18:32.420 --> 00:18:35.619
So again, if you have a game

369
00:18:35.619 --> 00:18:38.259
where you need different kinds of entities

370
00:18:38.259 --> 00:18:40.579
that can navigate the space in different ways,

371
00:18:41.700 --> 00:18:44.900
that's a really good candidate for using multiple kinds of agents.

372
00:18:46.339 --> 00:18:49.460
And you essentially would just create a new agent here,

373
00:18:49.460 --> 00:18:50.660
give the agent a name,

374
00:18:51.859 --> 00:18:54.099
and then you can just define settings about it.

375
00:18:54.099 --> 00:18:57.940
So how, what would the default radius for this agent be?

376
00:18:57.940 --> 00:19:00.660
What would the default height for this agent be?

377
00:19:01.299 --> 00:19:03.299
Step height, so, you know,

378
00:19:03.299 --> 00:19:05.619
if we're considering like a child versus an adult,

379
00:19:05.619 --> 00:19:09.940
they might be able to treat different vertical differences in geometry

380
00:19:09.940 --> 00:19:12.500
as kind of acceptable to navigate over.

381
00:19:12.500 --> 00:19:15.299
And so this is a, you can define a height radius.

382
00:19:17.059 --> 00:19:21.059
You can even define whether or not this particular agent can jump at all,

383
00:19:21.059 --> 00:19:24.259
whether they can crouch, that can affect the nav generation.

384
00:19:24.259 --> 00:19:27.940
And you can also create your own nav mesh classes.

385
00:19:27.940 --> 00:19:34.420
So a particular agent can basically define a particular nav mesh data class

386
00:19:34.420 --> 00:19:37.380
is used when creating the nav mesh for that agent.

387
00:19:38.660 --> 00:19:41.539
So I've actually just in the C++ for this project,

388
00:19:41.539 --> 00:19:43.779
I've just done a very simple example.

389
00:19:43.779 --> 00:19:45.940
I've not actually done anything with it,

390
00:19:45.940 --> 00:19:50.819
but I've just created a new class that inherits from recast nav mesh,

391
00:19:51.700 --> 00:19:53.380
just to kind of show that you can do this.

392
00:19:54.180 --> 00:19:56.019
That does require, if you were to do this,

393
00:19:56.019 --> 00:19:58.819
you just need to bear in mind that in your build CS,

394
00:19:59.619 --> 00:20:02.740
you do need to include the navigation system

395
00:20:02.740 --> 00:20:06.500
because that's what will contain the nav mesh actor.

396
00:20:07.940 --> 00:20:09.619
But if you create a separate class,

397
00:20:09.619 --> 00:20:15.059
you can now choose a preferred nav mesh data that actor will use.

398
00:20:15.619 --> 00:20:19.380
And again, I think as this says, you can also specify none,

399
00:20:19.380 --> 00:20:21.859
and then it will just choose any nav data it doesn't mind.

400
00:20:21.859 --> 00:20:24.420
And you can also say for this particular agent,

401
00:20:24.420 --> 00:20:27.460
specifically generate a nav mesh using this class.

402
00:20:28.480 --> 00:20:32.559
So that way you can have agents that require a particular class type

403
00:20:32.559 --> 00:20:36.559
and other agents that can use nav mesh data of that type,

404
00:20:36.559 --> 00:20:40.319
but also can tolerate multiple types.

405
00:20:42.160 --> 00:20:44.000
And so when you create those different agents,

406
00:20:44.000 --> 00:20:48.240
one thing to bear in mind is that if you don't have different settings,

407
00:20:48.240 --> 00:20:51.920
if you don't either have like different agent height or different agent radius,

408
00:20:51.920 --> 00:20:54.319
when you go to drag those volumes in,

409
00:20:54.400 --> 00:20:57.839
you may not end up with multiple recast nav meshes.

410
00:20:57.839 --> 00:20:59.440
So if you create different agents

411
00:20:59.440 --> 00:21:02.720
and it seems like you're only ever getting one recast nav mesh data,

412
00:21:02.720 --> 00:21:03.599
you actually need to make sure

413
00:21:03.599 --> 00:21:06.240
that there are meaningful differences between your agents.

414
00:21:06.240 --> 00:21:08.319
Otherwise Unreal will, I guess,

415
00:21:09.119 --> 00:21:13.759
just infer that they're so similar that they can share a recast nav mesh data.

416
00:21:14.960 --> 00:21:17.200
So just make sure that there are actually meaningful differences

417
00:21:17.200 --> 00:21:18.400
between your agents,

418
00:21:18.400 --> 00:21:21.039
such as different radius or different heights or something like that.

419
00:21:21.039 --> 00:21:23.920
Otherwise you won't get multiple recast nav meshes.

420
00:21:23.920 --> 00:21:26.960
And then for each of your bounds volumes,

421
00:21:26.960 --> 00:21:28.000
you can then say,

422
00:21:30.269 --> 00:21:34.109
is this bounds volume only for agents of a particular type?

423
00:21:34.109 --> 00:21:36.269
So there will be a tick box

424
00:21:36.269 --> 00:21:39.150
for each of the agent profiles that you've defined.

425
00:21:39.150 --> 00:21:41.390
The default is that it supports all,

426
00:21:41.390 --> 00:21:45.309
but if this nav mesh should only be generated for the common one

427
00:21:45.309 --> 00:21:46.190
rather than the big one,

428
00:21:46.829 --> 00:21:49.230
I can tick that off and you can see that gets regenerated.

429
00:21:50.430 --> 00:21:52.269
And then that nav mesh data,

430
00:21:52.269 --> 00:21:59.869
this whole area will only be navigable by agents using the common agent type

431
00:21:59.869 --> 00:22:01.150
rather than the big agent type.

432
00:22:01.150 --> 00:22:02.910
So that's kind of just another thing to be aware of

433
00:22:02.910 --> 00:22:04.430
when we're dealing with agent profiles.

434
00:22:06.420 --> 00:22:08.819
So nav link proxies, as I've mentioned,

435
00:22:08.819 --> 00:22:13.700
give us a way to connect independent areas.

436
00:22:14.980 --> 00:22:18.180
The kind of first example would be something like jumps.

437
00:22:18.180 --> 00:22:22.980
It gives us a way to jump from a ledge back down to the rest of the graph,

438
00:22:23.059 --> 00:22:28.259
but it also gives us a way to connect up a kind of isolated islands of space.

439
00:22:28.259 --> 00:22:33.140
So if I were to have two spaces like this,

440
00:22:33.140 --> 00:22:36.900
the way you would do this is there is an actor called a nav link proxy.

441
00:22:36.900 --> 00:22:40.500
I can never remember if you can find it through the all tab.

442
00:22:40.500 --> 00:22:43.619
If I go nav link proxy,

443
00:22:44.779 --> 00:22:46.859
that's finding a blueprint that I've made.

444
00:22:46.859 --> 00:22:51.259
Yeah, it doesn't for some reason, but there is probably another way to do this.

445
00:22:51.259 --> 00:22:54.779
But you can also just go to your all,

446
00:22:54.779 --> 00:22:59.150
and if I just search nav link proxy, you can find that here.

447
00:22:59.150 --> 00:23:00.349
And then this is an actor.

448
00:23:00.349 --> 00:23:03.230
This is essentially what a nav link is, is this actor.

449
00:23:03.950 --> 00:23:06.750
So you would drag this round into world,

450
00:23:06.750 --> 00:23:10.910
and essentially you just need to arrange this appropriately

451
00:23:10.910 --> 00:23:15.230
such that it actually links these two bits of geometry.

452
00:23:16.509 --> 00:23:19.630
So if I were to quickly demonstrate this,

453
00:23:19.630 --> 00:23:23.150
in fact, what I'll do is I've got an example map.

454
00:23:24.029 --> 00:23:25.869
We'll revisit this example map,

455
00:23:25.869 --> 00:23:28.589
because it's demonstrating a couple of different things at once.

456
00:23:29.470 --> 00:23:33.150
But if I just go to our example map,

457
00:23:33.150 --> 00:23:36.509
we've got kind of two islands of space here, right?

458
00:23:36.509 --> 00:23:40.289
We have these two navigable spaces.

459
00:23:40.289 --> 00:23:45.410
And if I've kind of set up some stuff in the level blueprint

460
00:23:45.410 --> 00:23:49.970
that essentially just says, when I press the three button,

461
00:23:50.690 --> 00:23:57.329
order this AI to try and move to where this point is.

462
00:23:57.329 --> 00:24:01.490
So if I just play and hit three, you can see it tries to get there,

463
00:24:01.490 --> 00:24:02.450
but it doesn't get very far.

464
00:24:02.450 --> 00:24:07.009
It gets to about here, it gets to the edge of its navigable space

465
00:24:07.009 --> 00:24:09.490
before it's like, well, I just can't reach that point anymore.

466
00:24:09.490 --> 00:24:11.809
I'm as close as I possibly can be, but I can't reach there.

467
00:24:12.990 --> 00:24:17.230
So if we put in, I've got a pre-made nav proxy here.

468
00:24:17.230 --> 00:24:19.470
If I just kind of snap this down to the floor,

469
00:24:19.470 --> 00:24:21.230
you can see the nav mesh regenerates.

470
00:24:21.230 --> 00:24:24.509
And that's because I've made a kind of like a link

471
00:24:24.509 --> 00:24:25.869
between these two islands.

472
00:24:26.430 --> 00:24:29.710
This is a specific actor that I've made,

473
00:24:29.710 --> 00:24:32.109
but really this just inherits from nav link proxy.

474
00:24:32.109 --> 00:24:34.829
I'll explain why I've made that nav link in a minute,

475
00:24:35.390 --> 00:24:36.990
why I've made that actor in a second.

476
00:24:37.950 --> 00:24:42.349
But just for now, you can see now that I've connected,

477
00:24:42.349 --> 00:24:44.190
now that I've brought that nav link down

478
00:24:44.190 --> 00:24:45.869
and kind of connected it up with the mesh,

479
00:24:47.069 --> 00:24:50.349
it's now like treated as a kind of valid connection.

480
00:24:50.349 --> 00:24:52.910
So if I were to kind of lift that up and away,

481
00:24:54.339 --> 00:24:56.819
I don't know how close it has to be exactly,

482
00:24:56.819 --> 00:24:59.940
but you can kind of see the arrow gets generated, right?

483
00:24:59.940 --> 00:25:03.140
When it's close enough, when it's no longer close enough.

484
00:25:07.420 --> 00:25:08.779
That's just hanging around now.

485
00:25:10.059 --> 00:25:14.559
Um, if I just wanted to play, sorry,

486
00:25:14.559 --> 00:25:16.319
just clicking the wrong things.

487
00:25:16.319 --> 00:25:17.759
Yeah, you can see that that's, um,

488
00:25:18.559 --> 00:25:21.920
for some reason, the debug arrow is kind of hanging around there,

489
00:25:21.920 --> 00:25:24.400
but that's no longer navigable space.

490
00:25:24.400 --> 00:25:28.319
And there's a certain point at which this is now kind of like,

491
00:25:28.960 --> 00:25:31.039
and you can see the nav mesh reacting to it, right?

492
00:25:31.039 --> 00:25:33.279
So it's too far away.

493
00:25:33.279 --> 00:25:38.160
Won't be treated as kind of an acceptable link.

494
00:25:38.160 --> 00:25:41.569
And once we're close enough, now it is.

495
00:25:42.289 --> 00:25:47.809
So the way you work with a nav link proxy is you,

496
00:25:47.809 --> 00:25:49.970
again, this is just an actor you can drag into the level.

497
00:25:49.970 --> 00:25:51.650
You can make blueprints that inherit from it,

498
00:25:51.650 --> 00:25:52.609
as you can see that I've done.

499
00:25:53.630 --> 00:25:56.349
And then essentially it's just these two point links.

500
00:25:57.150 --> 00:26:00.430
A nav link can also represent multiple links at one time.

501
00:26:00.990 --> 00:26:04.750
So it's not just a one-to-one.

502
00:26:04.750 --> 00:26:08.269
You can have a nav link that kind of represents a web of connections,

503
00:26:08.269 --> 00:26:10.910
but by default, it's going to be just two points.

504
00:26:11.789 --> 00:26:14.029
And it's got like a kind of left point and a right point,

505
00:26:14.029 --> 00:26:16.829
and you essentially just manually author these points.

506
00:26:16.829 --> 00:26:19.789
So you'll see, as I change these values, I've got this,

507
00:26:19.789 --> 00:26:21.950
you can literally see the connection changing.

508
00:26:23.869 --> 00:26:26.750
So you would just, if this is meant to represent a jump,

509
00:26:26.750 --> 00:26:29.549
obviously you could just manually author this to be like,

510
00:26:29.549 --> 00:26:32.029
have a certain amount of vertical transition between them.

511
00:26:33.470 --> 00:26:34.750
And that's kind of all there is to it.

512
00:26:34.750 --> 00:26:38.349
Then you can similarly mark up here, which agents is it suitable for?

513
00:26:39.309 --> 00:26:42.029
How far should it project up and down?

514
00:26:42.029 --> 00:26:44.349
So that's essentially going to be like, you know,

515
00:26:44.349 --> 00:26:46.269
if it's a little bit above the nav mesh,

516
00:26:46.269 --> 00:26:48.349
is it still going to try and find a nav mesh

517
00:26:48.349 --> 00:26:51.549
within this much vertical distance that it can be a suitable link for?

518
00:26:53.630 --> 00:26:56.910
And then also this idea that the, you know, is it a nav link?

519
00:26:56.910 --> 00:26:58.349
Is it one directional?

520
00:26:58.349 --> 00:26:59.390
So something like a jump,

521
00:26:59.390 --> 00:27:02.750
obviously you shouldn't be able to navigate back upwards up that cliff.

522
00:27:02.750 --> 00:27:04.349
It's kind of one directional.

523
00:27:04.349 --> 00:27:07.630
So you can choose, is this a nav link that is suitable for both directions,

524
00:27:07.630 --> 00:27:09.549
or is it left to right or right to left?

525
00:27:10.349 --> 00:27:13.869
And I suspect we'll get an update in the arrow that kind of demonstrates that,

526
00:27:13.869 --> 00:27:16.430
that it will kind of only work that one way.

527
00:27:16.430 --> 00:27:18.349
Now, the other thing to mention about this,

528
00:27:18.349 --> 00:27:21.390
and this is the kind of like reason I've made a blueprint here,

529
00:27:21.390 --> 00:27:25.150
is we've got this kind of more complicated form of nav links,

530
00:27:25.150 --> 00:27:27.230
of nav link proxies, which is called smart links.

531
00:27:27.789 --> 00:27:32.269
So with simple nav links, with the kind of standard nav link,

532
00:27:32.269 --> 00:27:36.269
that's kind of static data that is built in at edit time.

533
00:27:36.269 --> 00:27:38.910
If we need something that is a little bit more nuanced,

534
00:27:38.910 --> 00:27:42.109
a little bit more dynamic, that's where smart links come in.

535
00:27:42.109 --> 00:27:45.470
Smart links basically give us the same capabilities of nav link,

536
00:27:45.470 --> 00:27:47.230
they are part of the nav link proxy,

537
00:27:47.950 --> 00:27:56.059
but they give us the ability to essentially turn this functionality on and off.

538
00:27:58.460 --> 00:28:01.099
If a nav link proxy is utilizing,

539
00:28:01.099 --> 00:28:05.420
because again, any nav link proxy actor you make is kind of both at the same time,

540
00:28:05.420 --> 00:28:07.579
they've got the simple link and the smart link category.

541
00:28:08.700 --> 00:28:13.660
If it is a smart link as well as a normal link,

542
00:28:13.660 --> 00:28:15.500
then it will prefer to use the smart link

543
00:28:16.220 --> 00:28:17.740
if they seem to have the same connections.

544
00:28:18.380 --> 00:28:24.859
You can copy the kind of functionality of the basic points over to the smart link.

545
00:28:24.859 --> 00:28:28.539
So you'll see they have these, when you look in the smart link section,

546
00:28:28.539 --> 00:28:31.099
we've got like a link relative start and a link relative end.

547
00:28:31.099 --> 00:28:34.779
This works in exactly the same way that our left and right do here.

548
00:28:34.779 --> 00:28:39.500
But if you've authored a simple link, then you need to convert it to a smart link.

549
00:28:39.500 --> 00:28:43.099
So if I change this connection a little bit here,

550
00:28:43.099 --> 00:28:45.339
you'll see there's just this handy editor button

551
00:28:45.339 --> 00:28:49.579
that essentially just copies that left and right data

552
00:28:49.579 --> 00:28:51.980
into our link relative start and link relative end.

553
00:28:51.980 --> 00:28:54.619
But otherwise, these do exactly the same job.

554
00:28:54.619 --> 00:28:56.779
You can see as I tweak this,

555
00:28:56.779 --> 00:28:59.339
you can see it's changing this second set of data,

556
00:28:59.339 --> 00:29:02.940
and this represents the actual smart link, not the simple link.

557
00:29:04.130 --> 00:29:06.930
So smart links, the same functionality here,

558
00:29:06.930 --> 00:29:09.170
you can affect which directions it's for,

559
00:29:09.170 --> 00:29:12.769
whether it's both, whether it's one way or the other.

560
00:29:14.369 --> 00:29:17.809
You can check whether or not it's for a particular area, classes,

561
00:29:17.809 --> 00:29:19.970
and then you can turn this link on and off.

562
00:29:19.970 --> 00:29:23.410
So you can see as I turn that off, we get this different arrow color.

563
00:29:23.410 --> 00:29:26.210
So this is no longer a usable link.

564
00:29:26.210 --> 00:29:29.650
Obviously in this situation, because we've also got a setup here

565
00:29:29.650 --> 00:29:33.089
for our default point links, then it's kind of both.

566
00:29:33.170 --> 00:29:36.690
But if I were just to delete that, this is only a smart link.

567
00:29:36.690 --> 00:29:39.410
This is not usable.

568
00:29:39.410 --> 00:29:42.369
So our actor would simply not be able to use it.

569
00:29:42.369 --> 00:29:46.769
And if I were to set that as enabled, now it can.

570
00:29:48.130 --> 00:29:50.369
But this is also a change that you can make at runtime.

571
00:29:50.369 --> 00:29:56.529
So if I were to, I can just get my smart link component

572
00:29:59.500 --> 00:30:03.019
and just do set link enabled.

573
00:30:03.019 --> 00:30:04.460
I can never remember the function name.

574
00:30:05.579 --> 00:30:12.609
A set is enabled.

575
00:30:16.509 --> 00:30:24.059
Might be valid.

576
00:30:25.180 --> 00:30:27.579
Yeah, sorry, I lose the terms.

577
00:30:29.259 --> 00:30:30.779
If I just look up smart link.

578
00:30:39.619 --> 00:30:42.099
Okay, sorry, it's built into the nav proxy itself,

579
00:30:42.099 --> 00:30:43.940
not the component, my apologies.

580
00:30:44.660 --> 00:30:46.259
But either way, set smart link enabled.

581
00:30:46.259 --> 00:30:48.500
This is the function on the nav link proxy itself.

582
00:30:48.500 --> 00:30:52.819
And you can use that at runtime to toggle whether or not that's enabled.

583
00:30:52.819 --> 00:30:54.660
So that's kind of one component of it.

584
00:30:54.660 --> 00:30:59.059
But the other kind of useful thing to be aware of is once you're using smart links,

585
00:30:59.059 --> 00:31:03.220
things can be notified that that smart link is either available or being used.

586
00:31:03.220 --> 00:31:07.619
So one of the things I've done when working on games myself

587
00:31:07.619 --> 00:31:11.940
is I've actually used this event that you can build into smart links,

588
00:31:12.579 --> 00:31:17.140
this smart link reached to let us know when that link is being used.

589
00:31:17.140 --> 00:31:21.089
So if I, you can see that we're getting a print string

590
00:31:21.089 --> 00:31:23.250
when then the agent has started using that.

591
00:31:23.250 --> 00:31:25.970
This same thing wouldn't happen if we weren't using a smart link.

592
00:31:25.970 --> 00:31:29.970
So that's unique behavior provided by utilizing smart links.

593
00:31:30.529 --> 00:31:35.170
And you could use that for kind of any number of useful bits of information.

594
00:31:36.529 --> 00:31:39.329
You could play certain animations in this context.

595
00:31:39.329 --> 00:31:42.369
In working in games previously, we've used it,

596
00:31:42.369 --> 00:31:46.450
we essentially made door actors that were smart links.

597
00:31:46.450 --> 00:31:50.849
And we got notified when the AI wanted to use this door,

598
00:31:50.849 --> 00:31:53.890
we would kind of lock that door off so other actors couldn't use it,

599
00:31:53.890 --> 00:31:57.329
so we couldn't have two actors passing through this door at once.

600
00:31:57.329 --> 00:32:00.450
And then we would kind of enter a pre-canned door opening animation

601
00:32:00.450 --> 00:32:04.609
to make the hand nicely marry up to the door handle and kind of navigate through that.

602
00:32:04.609 --> 00:32:08.529
So that's an example of how, of kind of all the different capabilities

603
00:32:08.529 --> 00:32:10.930
and power that these smart links provide us.

604
00:32:15.809 --> 00:32:18.210
And then nav modifiers, we've already touched on this a little bit,

605
00:32:18.210 --> 00:32:21.890
but just to actually kind of show you them being implemented.

606
00:32:22.529 --> 00:32:27.809
Very simply, the easiest way to introduce nav modifiers is if we were to,

607
00:32:29.250 --> 00:32:32.609
they can have an independent volume, so nav modifier volume here.

608
00:32:33.170 --> 00:32:37.970
And you can see, this is just by putting it here,

609
00:32:37.970 --> 00:32:39.650
this is kind of blocking off the geometry.

610
00:32:40.849 --> 00:32:46.049
When you create a nav modifier volume, you get to choose an area class.

611
00:32:46.049 --> 00:32:49.170
You can create your own here if you need to do something slightly different,

612
00:32:49.170 --> 00:32:52.369
but by default we have a null, which is kind of, you know,

613
00:32:52.369 --> 00:32:56.049
this, I'm blocking off this space as non-navigable,

614
00:32:56.049 --> 00:32:59.730
but we can also do obstacle, which is essentially an area of high cost.

615
00:33:00.529 --> 00:33:04.769
So what you'll see is if I were to put this nav modifier volume here,

616
00:33:04.769 --> 00:33:08.130
when I tried to get my agent to navigate from here to this point,

617
00:33:11.299 --> 00:33:15.059
it's actually going to kind of navigate around that space.

618
00:33:15.059 --> 00:33:19.740
So if I were to kind of put this like this,

619
00:33:24.079 --> 00:33:27.920
you see it kind of goes really far out of the way to kind of navigate around that space,

620
00:33:27.920 --> 00:33:30.240
because we've marked this as high cost.

621
00:33:32.000 --> 00:33:35.359
So nav modifier volumes just give you a very easy way

622
00:33:35.359 --> 00:33:37.599
to introduce the idea of like high cost areas,

623
00:33:37.599 --> 00:33:40.799
where the AI will choose if there's no other path.

624
00:33:42.240 --> 00:33:46.720
It lets you define spaces that are completely non-navigable,

625
00:33:46.720 --> 00:33:50.880
even though there is no kind of like other collision geometry that would block that.

626
00:33:51.279 --> 00:33:56.000
And yeah, it gives us the kind of capacity to kind of change our nav mesh.

627
00:33:56.000 --> 00:33:57.920
The other thing to kind of mention here,

628
00:33:57.920 --> 00:34:06.559
we already talked about the idea of having possibly dynamic spaces, dynamic nav mesh.

629
00:34:06.559 --> 00:34:14.610
So if I were to go to runtime, if I just move our nav modifier out here,

630
00:34:15.570 --> 00:34:25.389
and I were to just play, and then I eject, and I find my nav modifier volume,

631
00:34:27.440 --> 00:34:29.199
you'll just have to kind of trust me that it's there.

632
00:34:29.199 --> 00:34:32.559
Unfortunately we don't get the editor handles when we're in play and editor mode.

633
00:34:32.559 --> 00:34:34.480
If I were to snap that down to zero,

634
00:34:35.679 --> 00:34:39.280
you can see that that's actually like carving out this space at runtime.

635
00:34:39.280 --> 00:34:44.320
The reason that's working is because our nav mesh modifier

636
00:34:44.320 --> 00:34:47.039
is set to dynamic modifiers only.

637
00:34:47.039 --> 00:34:51.760
If I change this to static, and then I go to play again, same thing,

638
00:34:52.480 --> 00:35:00.269
go to nav modifier volume, and I were to snap that to zero,

639
00:35:00.269 --> 00:35:01.710
you'd see our nav mesh doesn't change.

640
00:35:01.710 --> 00:35:03.309
And that's because it's now in static mode,

641
00:35:03.309 --> 00:35:05.869
so it's kind of ignoring any dynamic changes.

642
00:35:05.869 --> 00:35:09.869
So that is kind of a unique power given to nav modifiers,

643
00:35:09.869 --> 00:35:13.070
is the fact that we have this kind of second or third state

644
00:35:13.630 --> 00:35:16.510
for our nav mesh where we can be fully dynamic

645
00:35:16.510 --> 00:35:18.909
and then obviously would pay attention to modifiers,

646
00:35:18.909 --> 00:35:21.789
or we can ignore other kinds of actors,

647
00:35:21.789 --> 00:35:23.630
only pay attention to nav modifiers,

648
00:35:23.630 --> 00:35:25.630
so we're gaining some of the performance benefits.

649
00:35:25.630 --> 00:35:28.349
We're not trying to consider every kind of actor,

650
00:35:28.349 --> 00:35:33.230
but uniquely allow modifiers to change our mesh at runtime.

651
00:35:33.230 --> 00:35:36.030
And then that's another kind of area where nav modifiers

652
00:35:36.030 --> 00:35:39.469
and nav modifier volumes can kind of give us kind of extra functionality.

653
00:35:43.650 --> 00:35:45.329
So on to the environmental query system

654
00:35:45.329 --> 00:35:48.769
that we've talked a lot about kind of how we're able to navigate space.

655
00:35:49.730 --> 00:35:52.130
But one of the kind of important things

656
00:35:52.130 --> 00:35:55.489
when we're talking about querying things about the environment

657
00:35:55.489 --> 00:36:00.369
is the ability to ascertain where we even might want to go, right?

658
00:36:00.369 --> 00:36:05.650
A kind of typical thing that you might see in maybe a third person shooter

659
00:36:05.650 --> 00:36:08.530
is you have an enemy who seeks out cover.

660
00:36:09.570 --> 00:36:12.449
So we've got the navigation which can get us to that cover,

661
00:36:12.449 --> 00:36:15.010
but how do we choose which cover to go to?

662
00:36:15.090 --> 00:36:18.530
That's somewhere where our EQS, the environmental query system,

663
00:36:18.530 --> 00:36:19.650
can kind of come in.

664
00:36:20.989 --> 00:36:24.750
So the environmental query system allows AI to gather,

665
00:36:24.750 --> 00:36:26.829
well, not just AI really, anything in the game,

666
00:36:26.829 --> 00:36:33.469
and it is powerfully used by AI, but it isn't unique to AI.

667
00:36:33.469 --> 00:36:35.550
It can actually be used in gameplay as well.

668
00:36:35.550 --> 00:36:39.150
It's basically a way to query things about the environment,

669
00:36:39.150 --> 00:36:45.630
to construct, to kind of ask questions about our environment,

670
00:36:45.630 --> 00:36:47.949
construct a bunch of tests that say, you know,

671
00:36:47.949 --> 00:36:54.190
I want to seek out the nearest suitable cover for this kind of enemy,

672
00:36:54.190 --> 00:36:57.630
and then choose the location based off that query.

673
00:36:59.139 --> 00:37:01.539
It's worth mentioning that whenever we're talking about EQS,

674
00:37:01.539 --> 00:37:04.719
it is blocked behind a plugin,

675
00:37:04.719 --> 00:37:06.880
so the environment query editor plugin.

676
00:37:06.880 --> 00:37:09.039
I think when you start a new project,

677
00:37:09.039 --> 00:37:10.719
usually this is enabled by default,

678
00:37:10.719 --> 00:37:12.800
but just to kind of highlight that,

679
00:37:13.519 --> 00:37:17.199
if we go to our plugins window and we do environment,

680
00:37:18.690 --> 00:37:20.050
environment query editor,

681
00:37:20.050 --> 00:37:22.130
again, I think it's typically on by default,

682
00:37:22.130 --> 00:37:25.090
but just know that if some of this EQS functionality

683
00:37:25.090 --> 00:37:26.610
doesn't seem to be available to you,

684
00:37:26.610 --> 00:37:29.010
just make sure you've got this plugin enabled.

685
00:37:32.750 --> 00:37:34.110
And then, yeah, essentially,

686
00:37:37.070 --> 00:37:39.869
kind of fundamentally it lets us make these queries,

687
00:37:39.869 --> 00:37:43.550
and we use a combination of generating points,

688
00:37:44.190 --> 00:37:47.150
querying that, filtering and scoring

689
00:37:47.150 --> 00:37:49.309
to give either the best result

690
00:37:49.309 --> 00:37:52.510
or a couple of different options for either, you know,

691
00:37:52.510 --> 00:37:56.110
randomly choosing from the best 25% or the best 5%,

692
00:37:56.110 --> 00:37:57.309
et cetera, et cetera, et cetera.

693
00:37:59.170 --> 00:38:01.730
Yeah, environmental queries can be called from Behavior Tree,

694
00:38:01.730 --> 00:38:03.090
can also be called from C++,

695
00:38:03.090 --> 00:38:05.730
and it can be run in Blueprint,

696
00:38:05.730 --> 00:38:09.090
so it's not purely for AI,

697
00:38:09.090 --> 00:38:10.849
it can also be used for gameplay.

698
00:38:10.849 --> 00:38:12.610
I've used it previously,

699
00:38:12.610 --> 00:38:16.130
for example, when generating points to spawn enemies at

700
00:38:16.130 --> 00:38:21.570
or points where you might want to spawn drops,

701
00:38:21.570 --> 00:38:23.090
maybe you don't want to spawn your drops

702
00:38:23.090 --> 00:38:24.929
on top of any pre-existing enemies,

703
00:38:24.929 --> 00:38:27.170
you could use an EQS to kind of evaluate that.

704
00:38:28.619 --> 00:38:33.019
So the three key components that go into making up the EQS system

705
00:38:33.019 --> 00:38:35.739
are generators, contexts, and tests.

706
00:38:37.679 --> 00:38:41.440
Generators are essentially the thing, the first step,

707
00:38:41.679 --> 00:38:42.639
the thing that we say,

708
00:38:44.659 --> 00:38:50.019
the kind of fundamental makeup of an EQS query

709
00:38:50.019 --> 00:38:51.619
is a carpet of points.

710
00:38:51.619 --> 00:38:55.460
We essentially generate a carpet of points around our world.

711
00:38:56.340 --> 00:38:58.980
It's the generator that's actually used to define

712
00:38:58.980 --> 00:39:00.980
where should this carpet of points be,

713
00:39:00.980 --> 00:39:02.579
how should they be laid out?

714
00:39:02.579 --> 00:39:04.260
So we have a couple of different classes

715
00:39:04.260 --> 00:39:05.780
available to us by default,

716
00:39:05.780 --> 00:39:07.780
we can generate those points in a circle,

717
00:39:07.780 --> 00:39:10.179
which is just, we define like a radius,

718
00:39:11.059 --> 00:39:14.019
and then a, you know, degrees around,

719
00:39:14.019 --> 00:39:16.579
or a kind of radians around, not even radians,

720
00:39:17.380 --> 00:39:20.500
centimetres around the circumference of that circle.

721
00:39:20.500 --> 00:39:22.900
We can do a grid where we specify essentially

722
00:39:22.900 --> 00:39:27.860
a kind of 2D cube, a 2D square,

723
00:39:27.860 --> 00:39:32.260
and we say every 50 centimetres wide

724
00:39:32.260 --> 00:39:36.179
and every 10 centimetres in depth, generate a new point.

725
00:39:36.179 --> 00:39:37.139
And then we have a donut,

726
00:39:37.139 --> 00:39:38.500
which is a little bit similar to our circle,

727
00:39:38.500 --> 00:39:41.059
but it's the idea that we might have multiple rings.

728
00:39:41.059 --> 00:39:42.820
So we kind of generate in rows.

729
00:39:43.940 --> 00:39:44.980
Yeah, we can talk about,

730
00:39:44.980 --> 00:39:47.780
we can look at each of these kind of actually in the editor,

731
00:39:49.059 --> 00:39:50.340
but we have a couple of different ways

732
00:39:50.340 --> 00:39:51.699
of generating these points.

733
00:39:53.139 --> 00:39:54.739
And they are essentially, again,

734
00:39:54.739 --> 00:39:57.059
used to produce these locations

735
00:39:57.059 --> 00:39:59.460
and it's this kind of carpet of points.

736
00:39:59.460 --> 00:40:01.619
And it's then that carpet of points

737
00:40:01.619 --> 00:40:04.260
that we perform the tests on

738
00:40:04.260 --> 00:40:06.659
to actually give us our final result from the query.

739
00:40:08.639 --> 00:40:10.960
And also, yeah, custom generators can be created

740
00:40:10.960 --> 00:40:12.000
in Blueprint and C++

741
00:40:12.960 --> 00:40:16.320
if circle, grid, donut aren't unsuitable to what we need.

742
00:40:17.119 --> 00:40:18.639
Contexts are an important element.

743
00:40:20.079 --> 00:40:21.679
The generators are what generate the points

744
00:40:21.679 --> 00:40:23.920
and the tests are what test those points.

745
00:40:23.920 --> 00:40:26.079
But the contexts are things that are used

746
00:40:26.079 --> 00:40:26.960
at various points,

747
00:40:26.960 --> 00:40:29.599
either within the generators or the tests.

748
00:40:29.599 --> 00:40:31.360
They're a kind of frame of reference.

749
00:40:32.639 --> 00:40:35.760
So you'll see as we kind of quickly diagnose

750
00:40:35.760 --> 00:40:38.079
some pre-existing EQSs that I've made,

751
00:40:38.079 --> 00:40:39.119
the kind of various points

752
00:40:39.119 --> 00:40:42.559
at which we can use the various contexts,

753
00:40:42.559 --> 00:40:47.199
but they kind of give us a frame of reference.

754
00:40:47.199 --> 00:40:51.519
A context can return either a single entity or an array,

755
00:40:53.039 --> 00:40:54.800
but it's just important to mention

756
00:40:54.800 --> 00:40:58.159
that not all things that might be used in that context

757
00:40:58.159 --> 00:41:02.800
are set up to handle a context that can return an array.

758
00:41:02.800 --> 00:41:05.920
Some of them are only set up to handle a context

759
00:41:05.920 --> 00:41:07.519
that returns a single item.

760
00:41:07.519 --> 00:41:14.320
So a context might be the actor actually creating

761
00:41:14.320 --> 00:41:15.360
the EQS query.

762
00:41:15.360 --> 00:41:18.880
That would be kind of one of the standard query contexts

763
00:41:18.880 --> 00:41:22.159
is querier, the person who is actually making this query.

764
00:41:22.880 --> 00:41:25.280
Another example of a context might be

765
00:41:27.969 --> 00:41:30.289
all the rooms in your dungeon.

766
00:41:30.289 --> 00:41:32.530
You might create a custom context

767
00:41:32.530 --> 00:41:36.210
that says, oh, use my room logic

768
00:41:36.210 --> 00:41:40.210
to return an array of all the rooms in my dungeon.

769
00:41:40.210 --> 00:41:42.849
And then I can use all the rooms in my dungeon

770
00:41:42.849 --> 00:41:45.969
around the generator or the tests for various reasons.

771
00:41:45.969 --> 00:41:49.969
So again, these contexts are kind of between the lines here.

772
00:41:49.969 --> 00:41:51.969
The generators and the tests are the key points

773
00:41:51.969 --> 00:41:54.289
and the context can be used in each of these situations.

774
00:41:55.309 --> 00:41:57.550
And finally, we have the tests themselves,

775
00:41:57.550 --> 00:42:00.429
which are actually used to determine

776
00:42:00.429 --> 00:42:07.230
which these tests are going to basically run filters

777
00:42:07.230 --> 00:42:09.469
and scoring on each of these points

778
00:42:09.469 --> 00:42:11.550
that the generator has generated

779
00:42:11.550 --> 00:42:14.750
to establish whether or not this particular

780
00:42:14.750 --> 00:42:17.789
and an individual point should pass the test.

781
00:42:17.789 --> 00:42:20.590
And if it does pass, if it does not get filtered out,

782
00:42:21.630 --> 00:42:22.670
how should it be scored?

783
00:42:23.309 --> 00:42:26.030
So if we were to, the easiest thing to do now

784
00:42:26.030 --> 00:42:28.030
is actually just quickly jump into an example.

785
00:42:28.989 --> 00:42:31.630
So if we go, this should be in your project

786
00:42:31.630 --> 00:42:32.590
if you've downloaded that.

787
00:42:33.710 --> 00:42:38.989
So in the EQS folder, we've got two different EQS's

788
00:42:38.989 --> 00:42:40.670
that I've kind of made here.

789
00:42:41.780 --> 00:42:48.099
So as we can see, I've made a donut

790
00:42:49.460 --> 00:42:52.179
and this is just going to kind of generate

791
00:42:53.059 --> 00:42:56.739
several rings of points around our context.

792
00:42:56.739 --> 00:43:02.340
And as I mentioned, our center is defined as an actual,

793
00:43:03.539 --> 00:43:05.139
this is where we would use our context.

794
00:43:05.139 --> 00:43:08.500
So we have, our donut is using a custom context

795
00:43:08.500 --> 00:43:11.619
that I've made, which is based off a target actor key,

796
00:43:11.619 --> 00:43:12.739
so a blackboard key,

797
00:43:13.699 --> 00:43:15.780
rather than the person who's asking the query.

798
00:43:15.780 --> 00:43:18.900
This is intended to generate points around the person

799
00:43:18.900 --> 00:43:21.699
who we want to attack, essentially.

800
00:43:21.699 --> 00:43:24.260
So that's coming from a blackboard key

801
00:43:24.260 --> 00:43:27.699
and that's what I've made a custom context for that.

802
00:43:28.880 --> 00:43:32.079
And then we've got our two, these are our tests.

803
00:43:32.639 --> 00:43:41.199
So our donut is going to generate around this particular context

804
00:43:41.199 --> 00:43:45.440
and then that's going to generate based on what our donut says.

805
00:43:45.440 --> 00:43:47.519
So we've got an inner radius and outer radius

806
00:43:47.519 --> 00:43:48.960
and how many rings we need to generate.

807
00:43:48.960 --> 00:43:52.400
So we're going to start at a radius of 100

808
00:43:52.400 --> 00:43:56.239
and we're going to generate three rings between 100 and 200

809
00:43:56.239 --> 00:43:58.800
and every ring is going to have eight points.

810
00:43:58.800 --> 00:44:00.880
And this is, that's just our initial generation.

811
00:44:00.880 --> 00:44:03.679
And then we're going to run these tests

812
00:44:03.679 --> 00:44:05.599
on each of those points that we've generated.

813
00:44:05.599 --> 00:44:07.280
So, you know, three lots of eights,

814
00:44:07.280 --> 00:44:08.719
we're going to have 24 points.

815
00:44:10.079 --> 00:44:13.440
First thing we're going to do is this path that exists test.

816
00:44:13.440 --> 00:44:16.000
This is a pre-existing test that exists in the engine.

817
00:44:16.000 --> 00:44:18.719
This is essentially say, can we nav to this point?

818
00:44:19.360 --> 00:44:22.320
And then secondly, what is our distance from the point?

819
00:44:22.320 --> 00:44:27.599
So as I said, tests can be either a test that filters points out

820
00:44:27.599 --> 00:44:30.400
or it can be a test that scores them, or it can do both.

821
00:44:31.199 --> 00:44:35.599
So in this situation, our path exists, purely filters them out.

822
00:44:35.599 --> 00:44:38.800
It just says, if we can nav to this point,

823
00:44:38.800 --> 00:44:43.280
if this point is on navigable space, then it would pass the filter.

824
00:44:43.280 --> 00:44:45.039
If not, then it fails the filter.

825
00:44:45.760 --> 00:44:48.719
And then this next test that we do is based off distance.

826
00:44:48.719 --> 00:44:54.639
This hypothetically could filter points if it's too far or not far enough,

827
00:44:55.280 --> 00:44:56.719
and it could score them.

828
00:44:56.719 --> 00:44:59.280
But in the context of this distance 2D,

829
00:44:59.280 --> 00:45:02.320
which is again another pre-existing filter,

830
00:45:03.280 --> 00:45:05.119
I've chosen to score it only.

831
00:45:05.119 --> 00:45:08.000
So I'm not going to filter any points using this test.

832
00:45:08.000 --> 00:45:09.119
I'm only going to score them.

833
00:45:09.840 --> 00:45:15.199
And I've said it should score based off 2D distance, not 3D distance.

834
00:45:15.199 --> 00:45:17.440
So I don't want to care about verticality here.

835
00:45:17.440 --> 00:45:20.719
And then I've just set some things about our scoring equations

836
00:45:20.719 --> 00:45:26.800
so that this is essentially saying that if we think that distance from the querier

837
00:45:26.800 --> 00:45:29.760
is going to go up, that number is going to get bigger.

838
00:45:29.760 --> 00:45:33.360
So the default scoring is going to be further away is better.

839
00:45:33.360 --> 00:45:36.800
So by inversing that scoring, I'm essentially saying closer is better.

840
00:45:36.800 --> 00:45:41.440
So I want a point that is closer to our querier.

841
00:45:41.440 --> 00:45:45.119
So a point that the querier being the person asking the EQS.

842
00:45:45.119 --> 00:45:48.800
So in this context, it's the AI asking the question.

843
00:45:48.800 --> 00:45:52.800
I want a point that is closer to the person actually making the query.

844
00:45:54.769 --> 00:45:57.329
So we can see here how we've used generators.

845
00:45:57.329 --> 00:45:59.809
That generator has used a context.

846
00:45:59.809 --> 00:46:06.849
Our path exists test has also used a context here.

847
00:46:06.849 --> 00:46:09.809
So the context here being that standard default context

848
00:46:09.809 --> 00:46:11.889
of the person who is actually making the query.

849
00:46:11.889 --> 00:46:13.090
So that's our AI.

850
00:46:13.730 --> 00:46:19.730
And this is asking, you know, whether a path exists to the point from this thing.

851
00:46:20.690 --> 00:46:23.889
And similarly, our distance, we've used the context again to say,

852
00:46:23.889 --> 00:46:26.929
I want the distance from the person asking the question.

853
00:46:26.929 --> 00:46:31.329
I could alternatively change the context to be our target actor.

854
00:46:31.329 --> 00:46:33.650
And then this changes the meaning of this distance.

855
00:46:33.650 --> 00:46:38.530
Then I'm asking what is the distance from the,

856
00:46:38.610 --> 00:46:43.969
I want the point that is closest to the actual target.

857
00:46:43.969 --> 00:46:47.090
Whereas this is saying I want to choose the point that is closest

858
00:46:47.650 --> 00:46:52.929
to the person asking the question to myself, the AI.

859
00:46:54.300 --> 00:46:56.460
So a kind of important thing to,

860
00:46:56.460 --> 00:47:01.739
it's all good to talk about all of this in context in kind of like broad concepts.

861
00:47:01.739 --> 00:47:07.099
But when we're working with this, the environmental query system,

862
00:47:07.099 --> 00:47:10.139
obviously at some point it's helpful to actually see some of this stuff, right?

863
00:47:10.139 --> 00:47:11.980
Because it all seems so abstract.

864
00:47:11.980 --> 00:47:15.659
So one of the important things to remember is that there is a very,

865
00:47:15.659 --> 00:47:19.340
very powerful Pawn that we can utilize to kind of visualize some of this stuff.

866
00:47:19.340 --> 00:47:21.099
And that's the EQS testing Pawn.

867
00:47:21.900 --> 00:47:29.420
The way to put one of these in your level is similar to our NavLink proxy.

868
00:47:29.420 --> 00:47:37.460
I don't think you can just find it unfortunately through the normal place actors.

869
00:47:37.460 --> 00:47:39.699
So there is probably some other way to navigate to it.

870
00:47:39.699 --> 00:47:47.380
But the way I typically do that is just I go to all and then just search for EQS Pawn

871
00:47:47.940 --> 00:47:50.019
and EQS testing Pawn comes up.

872
00:47:50.019 --> 00:47:51.699
You can just drag this into your level.

873
00:47:52.340 --> 00:47:53.619
And then this is just a Pawn.

874
00:47:53.619 --> 00:47:56.659
You'll notice whenever you do that, you'll get a,

875
00:47:56.659 --> 00:47:58.900
it will bring in its own AI controller.

876
00:47:59.699 --> 00:48:04.019
So if you need to delete this, when you go to delete your EQS testing Pawn,

877
00:48:04.019 --> 00:48:07.940
you'll get a complaint from your level because this EQS testing Pawn

878
00:48:07.940 --> 00:48:10.260
is being possessed by this controller.

879
00:48:10.260 --> 00:48:14.019
So if and when you do go to delete your EQS testing Pawn,

880
00:48:14.019 --> 00:48:17.059
just be aware it brings in its own controller and you need to delete both.

881
00:48:18.019 --> 00:48:20.900
But what you do with this Pawn is you essentially just say,

882
00:48:22.260 --> 00:48:25.539
you give it an EQS to visualize, to test.

883
00:48:26.340 --> 00:48:29.059
So you can choose from any of the EQS you've already made.

884
00:48:29.380 --> 00:48:34.980
And you can see that this would essentially just generate this carpet of points.

885
00:48:34.980 --> 00:48:40.659
Now this isn't going to be perfectly set up because this is relying on,

886
00:48:40.659 --> 00:48:45.980
if we actually again, go and look at this, go and look at this thing.

887
00:48:45.980 --> 00:48:51.260
It's actually, this context here is relying on this EQC target actor key.

888
00:48:51.260 --> 00:48:58.460
So that this context is reading from a key from our blackboard,

889
00:48:58.460 --> 00:49:02.940
which is relevant at runtime for this particular AI.

890
00:49:02.940 --> 00:49:06.860
But in the context of in editor time, this might not always be suitable.

891
00:49:06.860 --> 00:49:09.579
So it's worth kind of bearing that kind of stuff in mind

892
00:49:09.579 --> 00:49:13.739
that when you're doing custom contexts, you need to be aware

893
00:49:13.739 --> 00:49:17.420
that if you want to be able to debug this properly at editor time,

894
00:49:17.420 --> 00:49:19.420
it might be worth kind of accounting for that

895
00:49:19.420 --> 00:49:21.980
and adding in some kind of default behavior.

896
00:49:21.980 --> 00:49:26.460
So if I were, the way I can kind of like actually do that,

897
00:49:27.019 --> 00:49:29.500
I can just quickly demonstrate how we might do that,

898
00:49:31.579 --> 00:49:35.420
is I will just quickly do query a blackboard.

899
00:49:36.380 --> 00:49:42.300
But what I'll also do is if that value is for whatever reason null,

900
00:49:42.300 --> 00:49:47.340
if I'll check if that is null,

901
00:49:47.340 --> 00:49:50.699
you can see it defaulting to just say around the actor itself.

902
00:49:50.699 --> 00:49:56.380
What I'll change this to is instead I'll do get all actors of class.

903
00:50:02.139 --> 00:50:05.659
So this is the custom, this is the actual,

904
00:50:09.840 --> 00:50:12.719
in custom context that I've made, this is the actor that represents that.

905
00:50:14.000 --> 00:50:17.679
And you can see, not actor, sorry, the class that represents that.

906
00:50:17.679 --> 00:50:20.159
And you can see it's got these two functions

907
00:50:20.159 --> 00:50:23.199
for provide single actor and provide single location,

908
00:50:23.199 --> 00:50:26.239
and similarly provide actors set and provide location set

909
00:50:26.239 --> 00:50:27.519
if you want to return an array.

910
00:50:28.000 --> 00:50:32.079
So I'm just going to get all actors of class,

911
00:50:32.079 --> 00:50:35.599
and I'm just going to look for character,

912
00:50:36.699 --> 00:50:40.219
and I'm just going to return the first character that we can find.

913
00:50:42.460 --> 00:50:48.619
Better yet, actually my player here is represented by the BPAI player char.

914
00:50:59.300 --> 00:51:04.050
So I'll just quickly hook that up and then get actor location.

915
00:51:04.050 --> 00:51:07.409
Obviously this particular setup wouldn't work

916
00:51:07.409 --> 00:51:09.010
if there isn't one of those in your level,

917
00:51:09.010 --> 00:51:12.449
but this is just to demonstrate how you might handle this

918
00:51:12.449 --> 00:51:17.570
in a way that kind of appropriately visualizes in editor.

919
00:51:19.329 --> 00:51:25.539
One second, why is that not, is that actually finding the key?

920
00:51:27.860 --> 00:51:29.860
Maybe I just need to reassign that.

921
00:51:30.420 --> 00:51:35.699
Yeah, there we go.

922
00:51:35.699 --> 00:51:38.980
So obviously we don't know that this would be the target,

923
00:51:38.980 --> 00:51:42.820
but just to kind of give us a slightly better demonstration

924
00:51:42.820 --> 00:51:44.260
of how this would actually visualize

925
00:51:44.820 --> 00:51:49.699
if we don't seem to be finding this blackboard key on our blackboard,

926
00:51:49.699 --> 00:51:51.380
which we won't because we're not at runtime,

927
00:51:51.380 --> 00:51:53.300
so that blackboard key hasn't been assigned.

928
00:51:53.300 --> 00:51:55.460
We're just going to fall back to finding,

929
00:51:56.260 --> 00:51:59.380
trying to find a player in the level and getting the first one

930
00:51:59.380 --> 00:52:02.019
and pretending that that acts location is our target.

931
00:52:03.309 --> 00:52:06.750
So then that gives us a kind of slightly better visualization

932
00:52:06.750 --> 00:52:09.150
of what this EQS is actually going to do

933
00:52:09.630 --> 00:52:12.110
It's going to generate points around our target,

934
00:52:12.110 --> 00:52:14.429
but then it's going to score our points

935
00:52:15.710 --> 00:52:17.789
based off whether or not we can,

936
00:52:19.869 --> 00:52:22.590
based on what is closest to the person making the query,

937
00:52:22.590 --> 00:52:24.909
which in this case is the EQS testing porn.

938
00:52:24.909 --> 00:52:29.550
And then we can also see what happens if we move our actor here.

939
00:52:30.190 --> 00:52:33.630
We can see it's going to, maybe I need to refresh that.

940
00:52:37.380 --> 00:52:39.139
You can see that filtering taking place.

941
00:52:39.139 --> 00:52:41.300
So this is a kind of important thing to bear in mind

942
00:52:41.300 --> 00:52:44.980
with the EQS testing porn is that these red nodes with zero

943
00:52:45.539 --> 00:52:47.699
don't necessarily mean they're never going to be considered.

944
00:52:48.500 --> 00:52:51.380
If you're doing an EQS query where every point is valid,

945
00:52:52.340 --> 00:52:54.340
then this is still a valid point.

946
00:52:54.340 --> 00:52:55.780
It's just getting a score of zero.

947
00:52:56.500 --> 00:52:58.820
The only points that are actually getting filtered out

948
00:52:58.820 --> 00:53:00.900
are these hard dark blue ones.

949
00:53:00.900 --> 00:53:04.659
So the scoring mechanism goes from red to green

950
00:53:04.659 --> 00:53:06.099
based off how high the score is.

951
00:53:07.059 --> 00:53:12.099
The points that are hard getting filtered out by one of the filters

952
00:53:12.099 --> 00:53:13.699
are this dark blue,

953
00:53:13.699 --> 00:53:16.820
and rather than showing the score that they're receiving,

954
00:53:16.820 --> 00:53:20.099
they'll print out a bit of text

955
00:53:20.099 --> 00:53:24.019
to say which of these tests was actually filtering this point out.

956
00:53:24.820 --> 00:53:27.460
And that brings us back to kind of one last thing to bear in mind

957
00:53:27.460 --> 00:53:30.579
when you're kind of structuring these EQS queries

958
00:53:30.579 --> 00:53:35.940
is that it's good to be doing your filters first, essentially.

959
00:53:35.940 --> 00:53:39.460
If you can, going from like top to bottom,

960
00:53:39.460 --> 00:53:41.300
if you're kind of putting your filters first,

961
00:53:41.860 --> 00:53:46.019
that means that your scoring queries won't be trying to score items

962
00:53:46.019 --> 00:53:47.780
that are never going to be considered

963
00:53:47.780 --> 00:53:50.179
because a filter later is going to filter them out anyway.

964
00:53:50.179 --> 00:53:52.099
So that's just kind of like a little thing to bear in mind

965
00:53:52.099 --> 00:53:52.980
when you're making these.

966
00:53:55.539 --> 00:53:59.300
But either way, you can see kind of by what's been demonstrated there

967
00:53:59.300 --> 00:54:03.619
that that's just a way to make kind of intelligent decisions.

968
00:54:03.619 --> 00:54:05.619
You can imagine this same thing.

969
00:54:05.619 --> 00:54:08.739
As I said, I've done this for games in the past

970
00:54:08.739 --> 00:54:12.659
where I've used this to query all enemies in your current room

971
00:54:13.300 --> 00:54:15.619
and find a point that is,

972
00:54:15.619 --> 00:54:17.380
and then score them based off, you know,

973
00:54:17.380 --> 00:54:19.380
their distance from all of those enemies.

974
00:54:19.380 --> 00:54:21.219
So you can find a point that is most likely

975
00:54:21.219 --> 00:54:23.619
to not have an enemy currently overlapping it.

976
00:54:23.619 --> 00:54:28.579
And that's where we should spawn our item chest, for example.

977
00:54:30.019 --> 00:54:32.019
So you can use that in kind of gameplay context.

978
00:54:32.019 --> 00:54:33.619
You can use it in AI contexts.

979
00:54:34.019 --> 00:54:37.219
Yeah, we'll actually come to another example

980
00:54:37.219 --> 00:54:40.500
from one of the Unreal templates in a little bit

981
00:54:40.500 --> 00:54:42.900
where we're going to kind of stitch all this stuff together.
